the genetic material is stored in the  form of DNA in most organisms in humans  the nucleus of each cell contains over  three into ten days for nine base pairs  of DNA distributed over 23 pairs of  chromosomes each cell has two copies of  this genetic material this collectively  is known as the human genome the human  genome contains about 30,000 genes each  of which codes for certain proteins  although thousands of genes in the human  DNA code for proteins large stretches of  DNA in the human genome are transcribed  but they do not code for proteins these  regions are known as introns and they  make up about 95% of the genome  some of this non-coding DNA control gene  expression but the purpose of much of it  is not yet understood this is a  fascinating subject that is certain to  advance rapidly over the next few years  so we know that all the information of  the human genome is coded in the DNA in  genes in the form of nucleotide base  pairs how this coded information is used  to produce functional products like  proteins is known as gene expression and  it essentially consists of two important  steps the first involves copying of the  coded information in DNA to RNA molecule  which takes place inside the nucleus of  the cell and this is known as  transcription this RNA molecule then  leaves the nucleus and the information  of this RNA molecule is used to produce  functional gene products like proteins  and this step is known as translation in  this video we will take a brief look on  the process of transcription make sure  to subscribe for upcoming video on  translation you can also take a look at  our previous videos on DNA structure and  DNA replication links are in the  description below  so the process of transcription takes  place in two broad steps first the  enzyme RNA polymerase forms a pre  messenger RNA molecule this happens in  the nucleus by using base pairs  complementary to the base sequence of  the DNA template strand in the second  part the pre mRNA is edited by more  enzymes to produce a desired mRNA which  is the final product of the  transcription so let's look at the first  section of this process which is the  formation of the pre messenger RNA which  essentially takes place in three steps  initiation elongation and termination  now the main enzyme that is involved in  this process of transcription is the RNA  polymerase the RNA polymerase has the  main job of copying the information of  the DNA to a new RNA molecule RNA  polymerase builds the new strand from  the five prime to the three prime  direction adding new nucleotides to the  three prime end so the first step in the  formation of a pre messenger RNA  molecule is the initiation in which the  enzyme RNA polymerase binds to the  promoter region of the DNA now let's  suppose this is a gene that is shown in  the green that needs transcription at  the beginning of each gene there is a  sequence of base pairs known as the  promoter region of the gene the promoter  region serves as a guide which tells the  enzyme RNA polymerase where to attach  once the RNA polymerase binds to the  promoter region it separates these two  strands of DNA providing the  single-stranded template needed for  transcription only one of these strands  is used in the process of transcription  which is known as a template strand this  strand always runs from the three prime  to the five prime direction this is  because of the fact that we just  discussed that is that RNA polymerase  can only synthesize the new strand from  the five prime to three prime direction  so it can only use a strand of the DNA  running from the three prime to five  prime direction as a template as a new  strand that is copied is always copied  in the anti parallel direction so after  the RNA polymerase binds to the promoter  region of the DNA the second step in  this process is elongation which is the  process of copying the bases on the DNA  template strand to form  new complementary RNA strand the RNA  polymerase uses nucleotides according to  the base pair complimentary rules to  create RNA copy in the previous videos  we learn how the DNA is copied by DNA  polymerase to form an identical new  strand of DNA in that case the DNA  polymerase added the base thymine when  the template strand had the base adenine  and it added the base cytosine when the  template strand showed the base guanine  and vice-versa but in case of  transcription the RNA molecule that is  produced contains the base uracil  instead of thymine so there are no T's  in the base pair sequence of RNA  whenever the RNA polymerase detects the  base adenine in the template strand  it adds uracil in the forming RNA strand  so this is how the enzyme RNA polymerase  adds new base pairs in the three prime  end of the growing RNA chain and this is  known as elongation  the next step is domination so just like  we had a sequence of gene known as the  promoter region which signaled the  enzyme where to start  we have regions of the gene that  basically signal the enzyme where to  stop these signals are known as  terminators and the signal that the RNA  transcript is now complete once the RNA  polymerase reads and transcribe the  Terminator regions the newly formed RNA  transcript is released from the  transcription complex  so this was the first section of this  process in which the DNA is used to  produce RNA molecule this RNA is known  as the pre mRNA or the pre messenger RNA  the prefix is used here to show that  this mRNA is not yet ready to leave the  nucleus and start the process of  translation so the next section of this  process of transcription deals with how  this pre mRNA is converted to a mature  RNA molecule so imagine that you want to  publish a new book and you have printed  all the pages now you cannot sell the  book yet because the new book needs  covers both front and back also before  selling it it is important to check for  any blank or wrong pages that may have  been inserted these two processes  essentially represent how the pre mRNA  is processed the first step involves  addition of a cap and a tail to the ends  of this pre messenger RNA molecule these  caps and tails are short amino acid  sequences that protect the pre mRNA  molecule the free ends of the pre mRNA  are liable to attack from many enzymes  the cap and the tail covers these ends  the second step of this process involves  removal of any unuseful or junk  sequences that do not code for proteins  and these are known as introns this is  known as RNA splicing in which the  protein RNA complex known as the  spliceosome removes these non-coding  parts the rest of the DNA is then joined  again together to create a mature RNA  molecule so this was a brief look at the  process of transcription guys thank you  so much for watching this video if you  found this video and all the other free  educational videos on this channel  helpful you can now support meThe  simplified on patreon.com for literally  the price of a coffee cup this will  allow me to fund my work and make more  videos like this and will also unlock  some cool patreon only exclusive content  like behind-the-scenes of these videos  upcoming videos early notifications and  exclusive flash cards and handouts make  sure to subscribe us on YouTube for all  the upcoming videos and also make sure  to follow us on Instagram and Facebook  for flashcards notifications and much  more
 

